  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nAbove seasonal normal afternoon highs will be the theme of the \nextended forecast period, though a cooling trend seems to be in the \nworks as we head into the late extended forecast. But first, we will \nhave to settle for consecutive days of mid to upper 90s and lower \ntriple digit heat as the FA remains encompassed by a 596 dam upper \nlevel high through at least mid to late week. Considering \ngeopotential heights remain above climatological standards, NBM high \ntemperatures tying the record of 102 degrees at Childress, and just \none degree shy of the 99 degree record at Lubbock, seems likely. \nThankfully, the ridge begins to break down late Wednesday into \nThursday the upper level trough to our north continues to translate \neastward through the Canadian provinces. Simultaneously attempting \nto swing a \"cold\" front into the FA. Though models diverge on the \nevolution of this, with most models washing it out before it enters \nnorthern portions of the Caprock regions. Nonetheless, flow aloft \nwill begin to turn northerly, bringing in monsoonal moisture into \nthe region. depending on where the associated disturbances reside \nwithin the main flow, combined with frontal/convective outflow \ntiming, we could see chances for isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms return late Wednesday. As far as temperatures go, \nthere is a vast amount of uncertainty, specifically with Thursday \ntemperatures, which will be determined by lingering outflows which \ncould allow for brief northerly flow or even the southward moving \ncold front. That is, if it can maintain itself and track through the \nregion. As a result of this uncertainty, we plan to maintain NBM \nslightly cooler temperatures of about 3 to 5 degrees on Thursday for \nthe time being. Though, this may need to be changed to account for \nfrontal evolution in the coming days. Towards late week, the upper \nlevel high will have split into two, with the pair of upper highs \npositioned over the Desert Southwest while the other centers itself \nover the eastern FL coastline. As a result, geopotential heights \nwill fall over the area, although still above climatological normals \nfor this time of year. Despite this, a secondary trough translating \ninto the PacNW will shift the westward positioned upper high back \nover the region through the weekend. Which will allow for hot/record \nbreaking temperatures in the upper 90s to near heat advisory level \ncriteria to return Saturday. As far as precipitation chances go, NBM \nmentionable PoPs seem probable at this time considering decent \nmonsoonal moisture return Wednesday evening through Thursday \nevening, as moisture wraps around the base of the upper high and \ninto the region. Combined with lingering outflows and perturbations \nthrough the main flow, isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms seem to be possible. Though confidence remains low in \nthe coverage and timing of precipitation. \n\nBy the end of the extended, headed into early next week, our eyes \nturn to the potential for our first cooling spell across the region. \nThis will be in part to the departing upper high to the east in \nresponse to a deepening trough over the Central Plains as it \ntranslates eastward towards the Great Lakes region. As of now, both \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance is keen on this bringing us our \nfirst taste of fall with the potential for increased precipitation \nchances as flow aloft turns southwesterly and aids in monsoonal \nmoisture into the region. However, this is still several days out \nand we do not want to get anyone's hopes up at this point. With \nstill several days for model spread to change. Though this hopefully \ngives everyone something to look forward too in the coming days.",
